he three prohibited directions.\n\n* If it is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), the only points of \\(T_3\\) with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (for \\(c=1\\)), or \\((1,2)\\) (for \\(c=2\\)), or \\((1,3)\\) (for \\(c=3\\)). Hence a horizontal line contains at most two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n* If it is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), the only points sharing the same \\(x\\)-coordinate are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) (for \\(c=1\\)), \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) (for \\(c=2\\)), or \\((3,1)\\) (for \\(c=3\\)). Thus a vertical line contains at most three points, and the only vertical line attaining three points would be \\(x=1\\), which does **not** contain \\((2,2)\\) or \\((3,1)\\); consequently a vertical line never covers three points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n* If it has slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), its equation can be written \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  The condition \\(a+b\\le4\\) forces \\(c\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\).  \n  - For \\(c=2\\): only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  - For \\(c=3\\): points \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  - For \\(c=4\\): points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n\n  Hence the only non\u2011sunny line that contains **three** points of \\(T_3\\) is  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{\\text{diag}}:\\;x+y=4.\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\nTherefore, in any admissible configuration the unique non\u2011sunny line must be \\(L_{\\text{diag}}\\) and it covers exactly the three points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Remaining points to be covered\n\nAfter (3) is covered, the three points that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese three points must be covered by the two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma: any line through two points of \\(R\\) is non\u2011sunny  \n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(P,Q\\in R\\) be distinct. Then the line \\(PQ\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe possible unordered pairs are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,2):\\;x=1\\quad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\n&(1,1)-(2,1):\\;y=1\\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\n&(1,2)-(2,1):\\; \\frac{1-2}{2-1}=-1\\quad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three cases are precisely the prohibited directions, so the line is non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Consequence of the lemma\n\nBecause of the lemma, **no sunny line can contain two distinct points of \\(R\\)**.  \nHence each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(R\\), leaving at least one point of \\(R\\) uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Contradiction\n\nAll points of \\(T_3\\) must be covered, but we have shown that at least one point of \\(R\\) remains uncovered. This contradicts the assumption that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nTherefore, for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** set of three distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Impossible}}\n\\]"
