            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\),\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly two of the lines are sunny (hence one line is non‑sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines that can cover three points of \\(T_3\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line must be of one of the following three types:\n\n* **Horizontal**: \\(y=c\\).  \n  In \\(T_3\\) the possible \\(y\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,3\\).  \n  - \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1)\\) (2 points).  \n  - \\(y=2\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) (1 point).  \n  - \\(y=3\\) contains \\((1,3)\\) (1 point).  \n  Hence a horizontal line covers at most two points.\n\n* **Vertical**: \\(x=c\\).  \n  The possible \\(x\\)-coordinates are \\(1,2,3\\).  \n  - \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) (3 points).  \n  - \\(x=2\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) (2 points).  \n  - \\(x=3\\) contains \\((3,1)\\) (1 point).  \n  Thus the only vertical line covering three points is  \n\n  \\[\n  L_V:\\;x=1.\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n* **Slope \\(-1\\)** (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)): equations \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  Since \\(a+b\\le4\\) for points of \\(T_3\\), the only admissible constants are \\(c=2,3,4\\).  \n  - \\(c=2\\): \\((1,1)\\) (1 point).  \n  - \\(c=3\\): \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) (2 points).  \n  - \\(c=4\\): \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) (3 points).  \n  Hence the only slope‑\\(-1\\) line covering three points is  \n\n  \\[\n  L_D:\\;x+y=4.\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\nConsequently, **the unique non‑sunny line in any admissible configuration must be either \\(L_V\\) or \\(L_D\\)**, and it covers exactly three points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that remain uncovered\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(L_V\\) (i.e. \\(x=1\\)).*  \n\\(L_V\\) covers the three points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR_1=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is \\(L_D\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=4\\)).*  \n\\(L_D\\) covers  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nleaving  \n\n\\[\nR_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn either case we have a set of three points that must be covered by the two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma: any line through two points of the remaining set is non‑sunny  \n\n**Lemma.** Let \\(P,Q\\) be distinct points of either \\(R_1\\) or \\(R_2\\). Then the line \\(PQ\\) is vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently it is non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe list all unordered pairs.\n\n*For \\(R_1=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\):*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(2,1)-(2,2):\\;x=2\\quad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\n&(2,1)-(3,1):\\;y=1\\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\n&(2,2)-(3,1):\\;\\frac{1-2}{3-2}=-1\\quad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(R_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\):*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,2):\\;x=1\\quad\\text{(vertical)},\\\\\n&(1,1)-(2,1):\\;y=1\\quad\\text{(horizontal)},\\\\\n&(1,2)-(2,1):\\;\\frac{1-2}{2-1}=-1\\quad\\text{(slope }-1\\text{)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nIn every case the line belongs to one of the three prohibited directions, hence is non‑sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Consequence of the lemma\n\nBecause any line through two points of the remaining set is non‑sunny, **a sunny line can contain at most one of those three points**.  \nWith only two sunny lines available, at most two points of the remaining set can be covered, leaving at least one point uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Contradiction\n\nAll points of \\(T_3\\) must be covered, yet we have shown that whichever of the two possible non‑sunny lines is used, the two sunny lines cannot cover the three leftover points. This contradiction disproves the existence of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there is **no** set of three distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Impossible}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
