 thus \\((a,b)\\in D_i\\).\nConsequently the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover \\(T_n\\) and contain\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Pair\u2011replacement Lemma  \n\nLet \\(p,q\\) be distinct integers with \\(2\\le p<q\\le n\\).\nConsider the four points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,p),\\;B=(p,1),\\;C=(1,q),\\;E=(q,1).\n\\]\n\n\\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the diagonal \\(D_{p}\\) (since \\(1+p=p+1\\)),\n\\(C\\) and \\(E\\) lie on the diagonal \\(D_{q}\\).\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{p,q}=AB,\\qquad L'_{p,q}=CE .\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are  \n\n\\[\nm_{p,q}= \\frac{1-p}{\\,q-1\\,},\\qquad \nm'_{p,q}= \\frac{1-q}{\\,p-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p,q\\ge 2\\) and \\(p\\neq q\\), neither slope equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\);\nhence both lines are sunny.\nThe set of points covered by \\(L_{p,q}\\cup L'_{p,q}\\) is exactly\n\\(\\{A,B,C,E\\}=D_{p}\\cup D_{q}\\cap\\{x=1\\text{ or }y=1\\}\\).\nAll remaining points of \\(D_{p}\\) and \\(D_{q}\\) lie on the other two lines\n\\(L_{p,q}\\) and \\(L'_{p,q}\\) (they are the only points of the two diagonals that\nare not on the axes).  Therefore the two sunny lines together cover every point\nthat the two diagonals covered.\n\nThus the pair \\(\\{D_{p},D_{q}\\}\\) can be *replaced* by the pair\n\\(\\{L_{p,q},L'_{p,q}\\}\\) without losing any point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Realising any even number of sunny lines  \n\nStart from the covering \\(\\{D_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) (all non\u2011sunny).\nChoose any \\(\\frac{k}{2}\\) disjoint unordered pairs\n\\(\\{p_1,q_1\\},\\dots ,\\{p_{k/2},q_{k/2}\\}\\) with \\(2\\le p_j<q_j\\le n\\).\nApply the pair\u2011replacement Lemma to each pair.\nAfter the replacements we have\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(2\\cdot\\frac{k}{2}\\) lines obtained from the Lemma);\n* \\(n-k\\) remaining diagonals (those not involved in a replacement).\n\nThe total number of lines is still \\(n\\), and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered,\nbecause each replacement preserves the covered set.\nHence every even \\(k\\le n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Realising any odd number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\) and \\(k\\le n\\).\nPerform the pair\u2011replacement Lemma on \\(t\\) disjoint pairs of diagonals,\nproducing \\(2t\\) sunny lines and leaving \\(n-2t\\) diagonals untouched.\nOne diagonal, say \\(D_r\\) with \\(r\\ge 2\\), is still present.\nReplace this single diagonal by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_r:\\;\\text{through }(1,r)\\text{ and }(r,2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-r}{\\,r-1\\,}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(r\\ge 2\\);\nhence \\(S_r\\) is sunny.\nThe two points \\((1,r)\\) and \\((r,2)\\) belong to \\(D_r\\); the remaining points of\n\\(D_r\\) are already covered by the sunny lines obtained from the pair\u2011replacements\n(their construction uses exactly the same points on the axes).  Consequently\n\\(S_r\\) together with the previously obtained \\(2t\\) sunny lines covers all points\nthat the original set of \\(n\\) diagonals covered.\n\nNow we have \\(2t+1=k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, still totalling\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  Hence every odd \\(k\\le n\\) is also\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Exhaustion of possibilities  \n\nObviously the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines,\nso \\(k\\le n\\).  The constructions above show that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be achieved.  Therefore the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).**"
