e lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{three lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\\\[2mm]\n1 & x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;y=2\\\\[2mm]\n2 & x+y=2,\\;y=2,\\;x=2\\\\[2mm]\n3 & y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe first three families contain respectively \\(0,1,2\\) sunny lines\n(the slopes are \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) respectively for the non‑sunny lines).\nThe last family consists of three sunny lines (slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)).\nThus (**P\\(_3\\)**) holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Inductive step\n\nAssume (**P\\(_n\\)**) holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nWe prove (**P\\(_{n+1}\\)**).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines\ncovering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nAdd the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{n+1}\\) is non‑sunny, it contains precisely the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus\nT_n\\), and it does not intersect any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) in a new point of\n\\(T_{n+1}\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}\\) is a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines\ncovering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines; consequently exactly one line\nof \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny.  Without loss of generality that line is the\ndiagonal \\(D_r\\) for some \\(2\\le r\\le n\\).\n\nReplace \\(D_r\\) by the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\text{ through }(1,r)\\text{ and }(r,2),\\qquad\nS_2:\\text{ through }(2,r)\\text{ and }(r,1).\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are  \n\n\\[\nm_{S_1}= \\frac{2-r}{\\,r-1\\,},\\qquad\nm_{S_2}= \\frac{r-2}{\\,r-1\\,},\n\\]\n\nneither of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\