            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_j\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_j\\) are *sunny*, i.e. not parallel to any of the three directions\n   \\(y=0\\) (horizontal), \\(x=0\\) (vertical) and \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) satisfying (3)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nThe lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\) are distinct.\n\n*Step 2 – the remaining points.*  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any \\(V_j\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the shifted abscissa  \n\n\\[\na' = a-(n-k) \\qquad(\\text{so }a'\\ge1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b = a-(n-k)+b \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the set \\(T_n'\\) is in bijection with  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the configuration required for the parameter \\(k\\).\n\n*Step 3 – sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-\\bigl(n-k+i-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are sunny.  \nTheir intercepts \\(-\\bigl(n-k+i-1\\bigr)\\) are distinct, so the \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (4).\n\n*Case 2 – \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \nSet \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge1\\)).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), we have \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni = a' - b + 1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\) we obtain \\(i\\ge1\\); from \\(b\\ge1\\) we obtain \\(i\\le k\\).  \nHence \\(i\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), and the line \\(L_i\\) from (6) is available.\n\nNow compute the right‑hand side of (6) at \\(x=a\\):\n\n\\[\na-\\bigl(n-k+i-1\\bigr)\n   = a-\\bigl(n-k+(a' - b +1)-1\\bigr)\n   = a-\\bigl(n-k+a' - b\\bigr)\n   = a-\\bigl(a-(n-k) - b\\bigr)\n   = b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k},L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are non‑sunny, while the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are sunny.  Therefore the total number of sunny lines in the construction equals \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Completeness of the answer\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the construction above).\n\n* No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the total number of lines is \\(n\\) (inequality (3)).\n\nHence the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
