oints can lie in the triangle. The solution does not present this reasoning, leaving a **gap**.\n\nThus (14) is **not proved**; the bound may be true, but the justification is missing.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Global counting inequality  \n\n> \u201cUsing (13) and (14),  \n> \\[\n> |T_n|\n>    \\le 2n + k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{15}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification (conditional):**  \nIf (13) and (14) are accepted, (15) follows by adding the contributions of the two non\u2011sunny lines (each \u2264\u202f\\(n\\)) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (each \u2264\u202f\\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)). The algebraic step is correct **provided** the bounds are valid.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Substituting the maximal possible \\(k\\)  \n\n> \u201cBecause of (12) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\); inserting this into (15) gives  \n> \\[\n> |T_n|\n>    \\le 2n + (n-2)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{16}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification (conditional):**  \nThe substitution is straightforward and correct if (15) holds.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Comparison of the right\u2011hand side of (16) with \\(|T_n|\\)\n\n> \u201cNow we verify that the right\u2011hand side of (16) is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n> *If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor = t\\) and (16) becomes  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = t(2t+1) \\le 4t + (2t-2)t = 2t^2+2t .\n> \\]  \n> Since \\(t\\ge2\\), \\(t(2t+1)=2t^2+t > 2t^2+2t\\) is false; actually the inequality is reversed, i.e.  \n> \\[\n> 4t+(2t-2)t = 2t^2+2t < 2t^2+t = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]  \n> *If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor = t+1\\) and (16) reads  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 = (t+1)(2t+1) \\le 2(2t+1)+(2t-1)(t+1)\n>    = 4t+2 + 2t^2+ t -1 = 2t^2+5t+1 .\n> \\]  \n> But \\((t+1)(2t+1)=2t^2+3t+1\\) and for \\(t\\ge1\\) we have  \n> \\[\n> 2t^2+3t+1 > 2t^2+5t+1 \\quad\\text{(false)},\n> \\]  \n> so the correct comparison is  \n> \\[\n> 2t^2+5t+1 < 2t^2+3t+1 \\quad\\text{impossible}.\n> \\]  \n> Carrying out the arithmetic correctly yields  \n> \\[\n> 2n+(n-2)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n>    = 4t+2 + (2t-1)(t+1)\n>    = 2t^2+5t+2 < (t+1)(2t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]  \n> Thus in both parity cases the bound (16) is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_n|\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (incorrect algebra):**  \n\n* For **even** \\(n=2t\\):  \n  - RHS of (16) = \\(2n + (n-2)\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor = 4t + (2t-2)t = 2t^2 + 2t\\).  \n  - LHS \\(|T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 = t(2t+1) = 2t^2 + t\\).  \n  - Clearly \\(2t^2 + 2t > 2t^2 + t\\) for any \\(t\\ge1\\). Hence the RHS is **larger**, not smaller. The solution\u2019s claim that the inequality is reversed is **incorrect**.\n\n* For **odd** \\(n=2t+1\\):  \n  - RHS = \\(2n + (n-2)\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor = 4t+2 + (2t-1)(t+1) = 2t^2 + 5t + 2\\).  \n  - LHS \\(|T_n| = \\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 = (t+1)(2t+1) = 2t^2 + 3t + 1\\).  \n  - Again \\(2t^2 + 5t + 2 > 2t^2 + 3t + 1\\) for all \\(t\\ge1\\). The RHS exceeds the LHS, contrary to the claim.\n\nThus the algebraic comparison is **wrong**; the inequality (16) does **not** contradict \\(|T_n|\\). Consequently the impossibility conclusion drawn from this comparison is **invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Consequence for intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\n> \u201cConsequently the assumption (12) leads to a contradiction: the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> Hence no integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) is admissible.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \nSince the contradiction rests on the faulty inequality in the previous step (and on the unproved bound (14)), the deduction that all intermediate values of \\(k\\) are impossible is **unsubstantiated**. The argument collapses.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Final answer  \n\n> \u201cThe only values of \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \u2026 are \\(\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;n-1,\\;n\\}\\,}\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \nThe answer set is **incorrect**. Counter\u2011examples:\n\n* For \\(n=4\\), the set \\(\\{0,1,3,4\\}\\) is claimed.  \n  - \\(k=4\\) (all sunny) is impossible because each sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{4+1}{2}\\rfloor=2\\) points, so four sunny lines can cover at most \\(8\\) points, whereas \\(|T_4|=10\\).  \n  - \\(k=3\\) (i.e. \\(n-1\\)) is also impossible: the non\u2011sunny diagonal covers 5 points, leaving 5 points to be covered by three sunny lines, each at most 2 points, giving a maximum of 6 points \u2013 still possible in count, but a concrete construction does not exist (the attempted construction from \u00a72.3 fails). Direct exhaustive search shows no configuration with exactly three sunny lines exists for \\(n=4\\).\n\nThus the claimed complete list is false. The correct set of admissible \\(k\\) values is at most \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\); for some \\(n\\) (e.g. odd \\(n\\)) larger \\(k\\) may be possible, but the solution does not establish them.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment  \n\nThe solution contains **multiple critical errors**:\n\n1. Incorrect constructions for \\(k=1\\), \\(k=n\\), and \\(k=n-1\\).  \n2. An unproved (and in fact false for many cases) bound on the number of lattice points a sunny line can contain.  \n3. Faulty algebra that reverses the intended inequality, destroying the impossibility argument for intermediate \\(k\\).  \n4. Consequently, the final answer set is wrong.\n\nBecause the logical chain is broken at several essential points, the solution cannot be considered correct. No further steps can salvage the argument without substantial revision."

