n \\(n\\) is even).  \n     Write \\(n=2t\\).  Take the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n     The diagonal \\(D\\) contains the points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n     The remaining set is \\(T_{n-1}=T_{2t-1}\\).  \n     For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-(t-1)\\le d\\le t-1\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_d:\\; y = x-d\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n     As in the odd‑\\(n\\) case, each \\(L_d\\) contains exactly \\(t\\) points of \\(T_{2t-1}\\), and the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_{2t-1}\\).  \n     All these \\(L_d\\) are sunny, while \\(D\\) is non‑sunny, giving exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting inequality (4) together with the parity analysis shows that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.  The explicit constructions above demonstrate that every value listed in (5) is indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound for a sunny line\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWrite the direction vector of \\(L\\) as \\((q,p)\\) with integers satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\qquad p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The three excluded possibilities correspond exactly to the three families (2).)\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\) and \\((x+q,y+p)\\) are two consecutive lattice points of \\(L\\), then  \n\n\\[\n(x+q)+(y+p)-(x+y)=p+q .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(T_n\\) the sum \\(x+y\\) ranges from the minimum \\(2\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\)) to the maximum \\(n+1\\); therefore the total possible variation of the quantity \\(x+y\\) along \\(L\\) is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nConsequently the number of lattice points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in only one point, (4) is still true.  Hence **every sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting inequality\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\n*Each non‑sunny line belongs to (2); such a line can intersect \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points* (the longest column, row or anti‑diagonal has length \\(n\\)).  \n\nUsing (4) for the sunny lines we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k)\\,n}_{\\text{non‑sunny contribution}}\n   \\;+\\;\n   \\underbrace{k\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor}_{\\text{sunny contribution}}\n   \\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Consequences of the inequality\n\nWrite \\(s=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  Then (5) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nn^2-kn+ks\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = n^2-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\), (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\;\\ge\\;k\\,(n-s).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\nn-s=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n}{2}, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{n-1}{2}, & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert (8) into (7).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, (7) reads  \n\n\\[\nt(2t-1)\\;\\ge\\;k\\,t\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, (7) becomes  \n\n\\[\nt(2t+1)\\;\\ge\\;k\\,t\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[1mm]\nn,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for **every** \\(n\\) the values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) are impossible, because they violate (5) directly (the left‑hand side of (5) is then strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\n\nAssume \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd) and \\(k=n-1=2t\\).  \nRemove one non‑sunny line (necessarily of type (2)); the remaining \\(2t\\) sunny lines must cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}=T_{2t}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(T_{2t}\\) the bound (4) gives at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{2t+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=t\\) points per sunny line.  \nHence the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2t\\cdot t=2t^{2}\\) distinct points, whereas  \n\n\\[\n|T_{2t}|=\\frac{2t(2t+1)}2=t(2t+1)=2t^{2}+t .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2t^{2}<2t^{2}+t\\) for every \\(t\\ge1\\), the covering is impossible.  \nTherefore **\\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur for odd \\(n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions showing attainability\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of lines for all values of \\(k\\) that survive the restrictions derived above.\n\n#### 5.1  \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  All lines are of type \\(x=c\\), hence non‑sunny.\n\n#### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \nAll points with \\(a\\le n-1\\) are already covered; the only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nAdd any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\),\n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1=\\frac{1-2}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n)\n   \\;=\\;-\\frac{1}{n-1}(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-\\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus it is sunny.  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n#### 5.3  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) – only for odd \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2t+1\\).  For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y = x-d \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_d\\) contains precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying \\(a-b=d\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le 2t+2\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\), the condition \\(a-b=d\\) forces  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le t+1+d,\\qquad 1\\le b\\le t+1-d,\n\\]\n\nso there are exactly \\(t+1=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) such points.  \nThe \\(2t+1=n\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d=-t}^{t}\\) are distinct, all have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny), and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 5.4  \\(k=n-1\\) (all but one line sunny) – only for even \\(n\\)\n\nLet \\(n=2t\\).  First take the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n\\(D\\) contains the points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points form \\(T_{n-1}=T_{2t-1}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-(t-1)\\le d\\le t-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y = x-d \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nExactly as in the odd‑\\(n\\) case, each \\(L_d\\) contains \\(t\\) points of \\(T_{2t-1}\\); the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_{2t-1}\\).  \nAll these lines are sunny, while \\(D\\) is non‑sunny, giving precisely \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final classification\n\nCombining the impossibility results (Sections 3 and 4) with the explicit constructions (Section 5) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem."
