            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n2. **Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line.**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(ax+by=c\\) with integers \\(a,b,c\\) such that  \n   \\(\\gcd(a,b)=1,\\;a\\neq0,\\;b\\neq0,\\;a\\neq b\\) (the three excluded cases give the\n   non‑sunny families (2)).  \n   The lattice points on the line are\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(x_0+bt,\\;y_0-at),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   where \\((x_0,y_0)\\) is any integer point on the line.\n   The sum \\(S=x+y\\) changes by\n   \\[\n   S(t+1)-S(t)=b-a .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Inside the triangle \\(T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le S\\le n+1\\); hence the total\n   variation of \\(S\\) along the line is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n   Consequently the number of integer points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the line is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|b-a|}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line is sunny we have \\(|b-a|\\ge 1\\) and, moreover,\n   \\(|a|\\ge1,\\;|b|\\ge1\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(\\pm1,\\pm1)\\) unless the\n   direction vector is \\((\\pm1,\\mp1)\\).  \n   In the latter case \\(|b-a|=2\\); in every other sunny case \\(|b-a|\\ge1\\) and\n   at least one of \\(|a|,|b|\\) is \\(\\ge2\\), which forces the step in the\n   \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate to be at least \\(2\\).  Hence the number of\n   admissible points cannot exceed the bound obtained when \\(|b-a|=2\\).  From\n   (5) we obtain the universal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }\\;s:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Counting inequality.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly\n   \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   *Each non‑sunny line belongs to (2) and can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points\n   (the longest column, row or anti‑diagonal has length \\(n\\)).*  \n   Using (6) for the sunny lines we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,n\\;+\\;k\\,s\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Writing \\(s=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) and simplifying (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\;\\ge\\;k\\bigl(n-s\\bigr). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n-s=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\), (8) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\ \\text{odd}. \\tag{9}\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Impossibility for odd \\(n\\) when \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Let \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd) and assume a configuration with\n   \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines exists.\n   Remove any non‑sunny line (there are at least two of them because\n   \\(n-k\\g