\(n\\) is ruled out by the same argument\n   (the non‑sunny line would have to be the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), which does\n   not belong to \\(T_n\\)).  Hence for odd \\(n\\) the only possible values are\n   \\(k=0,1,n\\).\n\n5. **Existence constructions**\n\n   *All \\(k\\) listed in (9) are attainable.*\n\n   - **\\(k=0\\).**  \n     Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  All are non‑sunny and\n     every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   - **\\(k=1\\).**  \n     Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\); they cover all points\n     except \\((n,1)\\).  Add any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) (for instance the\n     line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   - **\\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)).**  \n     Write \\(n=2t+1\\).  For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_d:\\; y = x-d .\n     \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(L_d\\) contains precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) with\n     \\(a-b=d\\); there are exactly \\(t+1=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) such\n     points.  The \\(2t+1=n\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d=-t}^{t}\\) are distinct,\n     have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny), and together they cover every point of\n     \\(T_n\\).\n\n   - **\\(k=n-1\\) (even \\(n\\)).**  \n     Write \\(n=2t\\).  Take the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n     \\[\n     D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n     \\tag{13}\n     \\]\n\n     \\(D\\) contains the points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) (exactly \\(n\\) of\n     them).  The remaining set is \\(T_{n-1}=T_{2t-1}\\).  For each integer\n     \\(d\\) with \\(-(t-1)\\le d\\le t-1\\) set  \n\n     \\[\n     L_d:\\; y = x-d .\n     \\tag{14}\n     \\]\n\n     As in the odd case, each \\(L_d\\) contains exactly \\(t\\) points of\n     \\(T_{2t-1}\\), and the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) cover all points of\n     \\(T_{2t-1}\\).  All these \\(L_d\\) are sunny, while \\(D\\) is non‑sunny,\n     giving precisely \\(k=