unny.  Using (2) for sunny lines and the obvious bound\n   \u201ca non\u2011sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\u201d we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,s+(n-k)\\,n\\;\\ge\\;N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Writing \\(s=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) and simplifying (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (4) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration.\n\n4. **When can all \\(k\\) lines be sunny?** \u2013  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, (3) becomes \\(n\\,s\\ge N\\).  \n   Because \\(s=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n\\,s=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\dfrac{n^2}{2}, & n\\ \\text{even},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2, & n\\ \\text{odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence an all\u2011sunny configuration is possible **iff \\(n\\) is odd**.  \n   For odd \\(n=2t+1\\) the following construction works: for each\n   integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x-d\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_d\\) contains exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a-b=d\\); there are\n   precisely \\(t+1=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) such points.\n   The \\(n=2t+1\\) distinct lines \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d=-t}^{t}\\) are all sunny and together\n   cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **General construction for admissible \\(k\\)**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the necessary condition (4).  \n   Put \\(r=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines we shall use).\n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Choose the \\(r-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r-1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are non\u2011sunny and are distinct.  \n   The vertical lines cover the whole columns \\(1,\\dots ,r-1\\); the diagonal adds\n   the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge r\\).  Hence the set of points\n   **still uncovered** is exactly\n\n   \\[\n   T_r'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge r,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is a right\u2011triangle of the same shape as \\(T_r\\) and contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_r'|=\\frac{r(r+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 sunny part.*  \n   The integer \\(r\\) has the same parity as \\(k\\) because \\(r=n-k\\) and \\(n\\) has a\n   fixed parity.  Consequently **\\(r\\) is odd exactly when \\(k\\) is odd**.\n\n   \u2013 If \\(k\\) is **odd**, then \\(r\\) is odd.  By the construction of \u00a74 we can\n   cover the whole triangle \\(T_r'\\) with the \\(r\\) distinct sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x-d\\qquad\\bigl(d=-(r-1)/2,\\dots ,(r-1)/2\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   These are precisely the required \\(k\\) sunny lines (because \\(k=r\\) in this\n   case).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(k\\) is **even**, then \\(r\\) is even.  An all\u2011sunny covering of an even\u2011sized\n   triangle is impossible (the bound (2) shows that \\(r\\) sunny lines can cover at\n   most \\(r\\cdot\\frac{r}{2}=r^2/2<N_r\\)).  To overcome this we keep the same\n   non\u2011sunny set as in Step\u202f1, but we **use one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines also to\n   cover a point that lies in a vertical column already covered**.  Concretely,\n   let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\;y=x-(r/2-1)\n   \\]\n\n   (this line is sunny because its slope is\u202f1).  It meets the triangle\n   \\(T_r'\\) in exactly \\(\\frac{r}{2}\\) points and also meets the column\n   \\(x=r\\) in the point \\((r,1)\\), which is not yet covered.  Remove that point\n   from the triangle; the remaining set is a triangle of size \\(r-1\\) (which is\n   odd).  By the odd\u2011case construction we can cover it with the remaining\n   \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  Altogether we have used exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n   all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   The two sub\u2011cases together show that for **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfy