on for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   The \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are vertical, horizontal or diagonal; each\n   of them meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Using (1) for the sunny lines we obtain the trivial inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot n\\ge |T_n| ,\n   \\]\n\n   which does not restrict \\(k\\).  The real restriction comes from the\n   parity count (2).\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is even and \\(k=n\\)* (all lines sunny), then every line can\n   contain at most one point with sum \\(n+1\\) (the largest possible sum).\n   Because there are exactly \\(n\\) points with sum \\(n+1\\), each line must\n   contain **exactly one** such point.  All those points have odd sum,\n   therefore each sunny line contains at least one odd\u2011sum point.\n   By the parity property of sunny lines (step\u202f3) a line whose\n   \\(|\\Delta|\\) is even would contain only odd\u2011sum points, while a line\n   with \\(|\\Delta|\\) odd would contain both parities, the numbers of odd\u2011 and\n   even\u2011sum points on it differing by at most one.  Summing over the\n   \\(n\\) lines we obtain that the total number of odd\u2011sum points is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\;+\\;\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{3n}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   because at most \\(n/2\\) of the lines can have \\(|\\Delta|\\) odd (otherwise\n   the total number of points would exceed \\(|T_n|\\)).  But by (2)\n\n   \\[\n   O_n=\\frac{n(n+2)}4>\\frac{3n}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge 4),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence for even \\(n\\) it is **impossible** to have\n   \\(k=n\\).  Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{when }n\\text{ is even}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For odd \\(n\\) the parity counts (2) give \\(O_n=E_n\\); the above\n   contradiction disappears, so no restriction on \\(k\\) arises from parity.\n\n6. **Sufficient constructions.**  \n\n   *All\u2011sunny case for odd \\(