ber of sunny lines is \\(k\\).\n\n   *If \\(r\\) is even* (i.e. \\(k\\) has the same parity as \\(n\\)), we first\n   use the same family \\(\\{S_d\\}\\) with \\(d=-(r/2-1),\\dots ,r/2\\) \u2013 this\n   gives \\(r+1\\) lines, one more than we are allowed.  Remove the line\n   \\(S_{r/2}\\); it is the line \\(y=x-r/2\\).  This line meets the triangle\n   \\(T'_r\\) in exactly \\(r/2\\) points and also meets the column \\(x=r\\) in\n   the point \\((r,1)\\), which is not covered by the vertical lines of\n   Step\u202f1.  Deleting that single point from \\(T'_r\\) leaves a triangle of\n   size \\(r-1\\) (which is odd).  The remaining \\(r-1\\) points are covered\n   by the \\(r-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_d\\}_{d=-(r/2-1)}^{(r/2-1)}\\).  Thus we\n   have used exactly \\(r=n-k\\) sunny lines and all points of \\(T_n\\) are\n   covered.\n\n   In both sub\u2011cases the construction uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   Therefore every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) is attainable.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (3) and the constructions of step\u202f6 show that\n   the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\]\n\n   This completes the solution."
