ording to the\ndifference \\(a-b\\).  Each line contains exactly the points with a fixed\ndifference, and the count of points on each line is \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  Hence the construction works and shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7. General construction for any admissible \\(k\\) \u2013 step\u202f6\n\n> \u201cLet \\(k\\) satisfy (3).  Put \\(r=n-k\\) (the number of non\u2011sunny lines we\n> shall use).  \n> **Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny part.**  \n> Choose the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=r-1\\) \u2026 and the diagonal line\n> \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\).  \n> \u2026 the set of points that remain uncovered is  \n> \\(T'_r=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge r,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\),  \n> which contains \\(|T'_r|=\\frac{r(r+1)}2\\) points.  \n> **Step\u202f2 \u2013 sunny part.**  \n> \u2026 *If \\(r\\) is odd* \u2026 we apply the all\u2011sunny construction of step\u202f6 with\n> \\(r\\) in place of \\(n\\): \u2026 These \\(r\\) sunny lines are distinct, all\n> sunny, and together cover exactly the triangle \\(T'_r\\).  Since we\n> have used \\(r=n-k\\) sunny lines, the total number of sunny lines is\n> \\(k\\).  \n> *If \\(r\\) is even* \u2026 we first use the same family \\(\\{S_d\\}\\) \u2026 this gives\n> \\(r+1\\) lines, one more than we are allowed.  Remove the line\n> \\(S_{r/2}\\)\u2026 The remaining \\(r-1\\) points are covered by the \\(r-1\\)\n> sunny lines \\(\\{S_d\\}_{d=-(r/2-1)}^{(r/2-1)}\\).  Thus we have used\n> exactly \\(r=n-k\\) sunny lines \u2026\u201d\n\n*Critical Error (miscount of sunny lines).*  \nThe construction **uses \\(r\\) sunny lines** (the all\u2011sunny construction for a\ntriangle of size \\(r\\) always needs exactly \\(r\\) lines).  However, the\nproblem requires **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**, where \\(k=n-r\\).  The\nsolution mistakenly identifies the number of sunny lines used with\n\\(k\\); in fact it uses \\(r\\) sunny lines, i.e. the *complement* of the\ndesired number.  Consequent