            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a **complete** solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of the form \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Parity of the points of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   For every \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) with\n   \\(a+b=s\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   E_n:=\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\text{ even}\\}\n        =\\begin{cases}\n          \\dfrac{n^{2}}4, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n          \\dfrac{n^{2}-1}4, & n\\text{ odd},\n        \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   O_n:=\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\text{ odd}\\}\n        =\\begin{cases}\n          \\dfrac{n(n+2)}4, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n          \\dfrac{n^{2}+2n+1}4, & n\\text{ odd}.\n        \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A property of sunny lines.**  \n   Write a sunny line in primitive integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   ux+vy=w,\\qquad u,v\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(u,v)=1,\\;\n   u\\neq0,\\;v\\neq0,\\;u\\neq -v .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All integer points on the line are \\((x_0+vt,\\;y_0-ut)\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\).  \n   The sum \\(S=x+y\\) changes by \\(\\Delta=S(t+1)-S(t)=v-u\\neq0\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line contains **at most one** point of each\n   possible sum \\(a+b\\).  Moreover, if \\(|\\Delta|\\) is even then the parity\n   of \\(S\\) is constant on the line; if \\(|\\Delta|\\) is odd the numbers of\n   even‑ and odd‑sum points on the line differ by at most one.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   Assume that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with\n   even \\(n\\).  \n   Because each sunny line meets each diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) in at most one\n   point, the \\(n\\) lines together contain at most \\(n\\) points whose sum\n   is odd **and** whose parity is constant on the line.  The lines whose\n   \\(|\\Delta|\\) is odd can contribute at most one extra odd‑sum point\n   each, so the total number of odd‑sum points covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n+\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{3n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   By (1) we have \\(O_n=\\dfrac{n(n+2)}4\\).  For even \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+2)}4>\\frac{3n}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Hence for even \\(n\\) it is **impossible** that all\n   \\(n\\) lines are sunny; i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n5. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the condition stated in the theorem and put  \n\n   \\[\n   r:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le r\\le n-1\\text{ if }n\\text{ even},\\;0\\le r\\le n\\text{ if }n\\text{ odd}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny part.*  \n   Use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (if \\(r=0\\) this step is omitted).  \n   These \\(r\\) lines are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\n   first coordinate lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,r\\}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \n   After removing the points covered in Step 1 the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T'_r:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge r+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating by \\((-r,0)\\) we see that \\(T'_r\\) is exactly the set\n   \\(T_{k}\\) (because \\(k=n-r\\)).  Thus we have to cover the triangle\n   \\(T_{k}\\) with the remaining \\(k\\) lines, all of which must be sunny.\n\n   *Covering \\(T_{k}\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   We treat the parity of \\(k\\) separately.\n\n   **(i)  \\(k\\) odd.**  \n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\).  For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x-d .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(L_d\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   hence every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) we have \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) and therefore\n   \\(|a-b|\\le t\\).  Consequently \\(a-b=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\) in the\n   interval \\([-t,t]\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding line\n   \\(L_d\\).  Hence the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d=-t}^{t}\\) cover **all**\n   points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   **(ii)  \\(k\\) even.**  \n   Write \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\).  First take the \\(2t-1\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;y=x-d ,\\qquad d=-t+1,\\dots ,t-1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   As in the odd case, each of these lines is sunny.  They cover every\n   point of \\(T_k\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) lies in \\([-t+1,t-1]\\).  \n   The only points of \\(T_k\\) not yet covered are those with\n   \\(a-b=\\pm t\\).  Those points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{+}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_k\\mid a-b=t\\},\\qquad\n   P_{-}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_k\\mid a-b=-t\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Both sets consist of \\(\\displaystyle\\left\\lceil\\frac{t}{2}\\right\\rceil\\)\n   points; in particular they are non‑empty.  We cover them with a **single**\n   additional sunny line\n\n   \\[\n   M:\\;y=-2x+(k+2).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(M\\) has slope \\(-2\\), which is allowed.  Direct substitution\n   shows that for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(t+1\\le a\\le k\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b=-2a+(k+2)=k+2-2a\\ge 1,\n   \\qquad a+b=k+2-a\\le k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and \\(a-b=t\\).  Similarly, for every integer\n   \\(b\\) with \\(t+1\\le b\\le k\\) the point \\((k+2-2b,b)\\) lies on \\(M\\) and\n   satisfies \\(a-b=-t\\).  Hence **all** points of \\(P_{+}\\cup P_{-}\\) lie on\n   \\(M\\).  The line \\(M\\) is sunny and does not coincide with any of the\n   lines in (6).  Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{L_d\\}_{d=-t+1}^{t-1}\\cup\\{M\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(2t-1+1=k\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the\n   whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n   Thus for **every** positive integer \\(k\\) we have exhibited \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines that cover \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – assembling the whole configuration.*  \n   Combine the \\(r\\) vertical lines (4) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained\n   in Step 2.  The total number of lines is \\(r+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them\n   are sunny.  By the discussion in Step 2 every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs\n   either to a vertical line (if its first coordinate \\(\\le r\\)) or to one of\n   the sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\) (if its first coordinate \\(\\ge r+1\\)).\n   Hence **all** points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-1\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\n   \\qquad\n   0\\le k\\le n\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\n   \\]\n\n   because for even \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is excluded by the impossibility\n   proved in step 4, while for odd \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is allowed and is\n   realised by taking \\(r=0\\) and applying the construction of\n   §5(ii) with \\(k=n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The parity formulas (1) are correct for both even and odd \\(n\\); the\n   parity argument in step 4 shows that for even \\(n\\) the all‑sunny\n   configuration is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) it is possible.\n   The explicit constructions in step 5 give, for every admissible \\(k\\),\n   a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\n   all required lattice points.  Therefore the set of admissible values\n   of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the theorem.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
