n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 – Definition of \\(T_n\\) and its cardinality  \n\n> “\\(T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n> \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\)”  \n\n*Verification:* The description matches the problem’s requirement.  \nThe count \\(\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is correct.  \n**No issue.**\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 – Parity of the points of \\(T_n\\)  \n\n> “For every \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) there are exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) with\n> \\(a+b=s\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nE_n:=\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\text{ even}\\}\n     =\\begin{cases}\n       \\dfrac{n^{2}}4, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n       \\dfrac{n^{2}-1}4, & n\\text{ odd},\n     \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\\[\nO_n:=\\#\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\text{ odd}\\}\n     =\\begin{cases}\n       \\dfrac{n(n+2)}4, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n       \\dfrac{n^{2}+2n+1}4, & n\\text{ odd}.\n     \\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]”\n\n*Verification:*  \nFor **even** \\(n\\) the formulas are correct (as shown in the analysis).  \nFor **odd** \\(n\\) the formulas are **incorrect**. The correct values are  \n\n\\[\nE_n=\\frac{(n+1)^2}{4}=\\frac{n^{2}+2n+1}{4},\\qquad\nO_n=\\frac{n^{2}-1}{4}.\n\\]\n\nThus the statement “\\(E_n=\\frac{n^{2}-1}{4}\\) and \\(O_n=\\frac{n^{2}+2n+1}{4}\\) for odd \\(n\\)” is false.\n\n**Issue:** *Critical Error* – a factual mistake that invalidates the claimed parity formulas for odd \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – A property of sunny lines  \n\n> “Write a sunny line in primitive integer form  \n\n\\[\nux+vy=w,\\qquad u,v\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(u,v)=1,\\;\nu\\neq0,\\;v\\neq0,\\;u\\neq -v .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The representation \\(ux+vy=w\\) with \\(\\gcd(u,v)=1\\) is standard.  \n- The conditions \\(u\\neq0\\) and \\(v\\neq0\\) correctly exclude vertical and horizontal lines.  \n- **Error:** The condition for a line **not** to be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is \\(u\\neq v\\) (since \\(x+y=0\\) corresponds to \\(u=v\\)). The statement \\(u\\neq -v\\) excludes lines parallel to \\(x-y=0\\) (slope 1), which are *allowed* sunny lines. Hence the description of a sunny line is mathematically inaccurate.\n\n*Further claims* about integer points \\((x_0+vt,\\;y_0-ut)\\) and the increment \\(\\Delta=v-u\\neq0\\) are correct **provided** the line is indeed sunny (i.e. \\(u\\neq v\\)). The subsequent reasoning uses only \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\), so the later steps are not directly harmed by the mis‑stated condition, but the definition itself is wrong.\n\n**Issue:** *Critical Error* – a factual misstatement about the defining condition of sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\n> “Assume that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) with\n> even \\(n\\).  \n> Because each sunny line meets each diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) in at most one point,\n> the \\(n\\) lines together contain at most \\(n\\) points whose sum is odd **and**\n> whose parity is constant on the line.  The lines whose \\(|\\Delta|\\) is odd can\n> contribute at most one extra odd‑sum point each, so the total number of\n> odd‑sum points covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn+\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{3n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n> By (1) we have \\(O_n=\\dfrac{n(n+2)}4\\).  For even \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+2)}4>\\frac{3n}{2},\n\\]\n\n> a contradiction.”\n\n*Verification of the logical chain:*  \n\n1. **Claim:** “Each sunny line meets each diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) in at most one point.” – This is true.  \n\n2. **Claim:** “The \\(n\\) lines together contain at most \\(n\\) points whose sum is odd **and** whose parity is constant on the line.” – **False.**  \n   - For a sunny line with \\(\\Delta=v-u\\) **even**, the parity of \\(a+b\\) is constant (all even or all odd).  \n   - If that constant parity is odd, the line can contain **many** odd‑sum points (one on each diagonal of odd sum). There is no reason to limit it to a single odd‑sum point.  \n\n3. **Consequent bound** “at most \\(n+\\frac{n}{2}\\) odd‑sum points” therefore rests on a false premise.  \n\n4. **Numerical inequality** \\(\\frac{n(n+2)}4>\\frac{3n}{2}\\) is **incorrect for \\(n=4\\)**: both sides equal \\(6\\). Hence the argument does not even produce a contradiction for the smallest even case where a counterexample could exist.\n\nThus the entire impossibility proof collapses.\n\n**Issue:** *Critical Error* – the bound on odd‑sum points is mathematically wrong, and the derived inequality fails for \\(n=4\\). Consequently the claim “\\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\)” is not proved.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Construction for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### (i) Odd \\(k\\)\n\n> “Write \\(k=2t+1\\).  For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;y=x-d .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]  \n\n> … For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) we have \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) and therefore\n> \\(|a-b|\\le t\\).  Consequently \\(a-b=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\) in the\n> interval \\([-t,t]\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding line\n> \\(L_d\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The family \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) consists of lines of slope 1, which are sunny.  \n- **Error:** The inequality \\(|a-b|\\le t\\) for all \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) is **false**.  \n  Counterexample: for \\(k=5\\) (\\(t=2\\)), the point \\((1,4)\\in T_5\\) satisfies \\(a+b=5\\le6\\) but \\(|a-b|=3>t\\). Hence \\((1,4)\\) is **not** on any line \\(L_d\\) with \\(-2\\le d\\le2\\).  \n  The same failure occurs for every odd \\(k\\ge3\\). Therefore the claimed covering of \\(T_k\\) does not hold.\n\n**Issue:** *Critical Error* – the construction does not actually cover all points of \\(T_k\\); the key inequality is wrong.\n\n#### (ii) Even \\(k\\)\n\n> “Write \\(k=2t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\).  First take the \\(2t-1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;y=x-d ,\\qquad d=-t+1,\\dots ,t-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]  \n\n> … The only points of \\(T_k\\) not yet covered are those with\n> \\(a-b=\\pm t\\).  … We cover them with a **single** additional sunny line  \n\n\\[\nM:\\;y=-2x+(k+2).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]  \n\n> … Direct substitution shows that for every integer \\(a\\) with\n> \\(t+1\\le a\\le k\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb=-2a+(k+2)=k+2-2a\\ge 1,\n\\qquad a+b=k+2-a\\le k+1,\n\\]\n\n> i.e. \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and \\(a-b=t\\).  Similarly … points with\n> \\(a-b=-t\\) also lie on \\(M\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The lines \\(L_d\\) are sunny (slope 1) and correctly cover all points with\n  \\(-t+1\\le a-b\\le t-1\\).  \n- **Error 1:** The set of points with \\(a-b=t\\) (or \\(-t\\)) is **not** given by the parametrisation \\(a\\in[t+1,k]\\) used in the proof.  \n  Solving \\(a-b=t\\) together with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) yields \\(b\\le\\frac{t+1}{2}\\), not the range claimed.  \n- **Error 2:** Substituting a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a-b=t\\) into the line\n  \\(M: y=-2x+(k+2)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=-2a+(2t+2)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 3b=2,\n\\]\n\n  which has no integer solution for \\(t\\ge2\\). For instance, with \\(k=4\\) (\\(t=2\\)) the point \\((3,1)\\) (which satisfies \\(a-b=2\\) and belongs to \\(T_4\\)) does **not** satisfy \\(y=-2x+6\\) (since \\(-2\\cdot3+6=0\\neq1\\)).  \n  Hence \\(M\\) does **not** pass through the required points for any \\(t\\ge2\\).  \n\nThus the claimed covering for even \\(k\\) fails except for the trivial case \\(t=1\\) (\\(k=2\\)).\n\n**Issue:** *Critical Error* – the extra line \\(M\\) does not cover the points with \\(a-b=\\pm t\\); the construction is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 – Conclusion  \n\n> “The parity formulas (1) are correct for both even and odd \\(n\\); the\n> parity argument in step 4 shows that for even \\(n\\) the all‑sunny\n> configuration is impossible, while for odd \\(n\\) it is possible.\n> The explicit constructions in step 5 give, for every admissible \\(k\\),\n> a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines … Therefore the set of admissible values\n> of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the theorem.”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The statement “parity formulas (1) are correct for both even and odd \\(n\\)” is false (see Step 2).  \n- Step 4’s impossibility argument is invalid (see Step 4).  \n- The constructions in Step 5 are shown to be incorrect for all but the smallest values of \\(k\\) (see Step 5).  \n\nBecause the proof’s core lemmas and constructions are erroneous, the final claim about the admissible set of \\(k\\) is **unsupported**.\n\n**Issue:** *Critical Error* – the conclusion rests on the previously identified false statements and flawed constructions.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains multiple critical errors: incorrect parity formulas, a mischaracterisation of sunny lines, an invalid impossibility argument for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even, and constructions that fail to cover the required lattice points for both odd and even values of \\(k\\). Consequently, the answer “\\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) for even \\(n\\) and \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) for odd \\(n\\)” is not established. The solution is therefore **incorrect**."
