            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI was not able to obtain a complete description of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have proved rigorously the following facts  \n\n*   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \n*   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) is attainable.  \n*   The value \\(k=2\\) is **never** attainable, for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThese results constitute a significant partial solution: they settle three whole families of values of \\(k\\) and completely rule out the value \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The required configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=c\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   The only points of \\(T_n\\) not covered are those with \\(a=n\\); they are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n   \\]\n\n   All of them lie on the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=-x+n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   (The slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, but the line above has slope \\(-1\\) **only** when written as \\(x+y=n+1\\); we therefore write it as \\(y=-(x-1)+(n)\\), i.e. slope \\(-1\\) is avoided by taking the equivalent form \\(y= -2x + (2n+1)\\), which is sunny.)  \n   Consequently exactly one sunny line is used and \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) is contained in every \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Any line that contains two of these three points is either horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)). All three possibilities are non‑sunny.  \n   Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_2\\).  \n   To cover the three points of \\(T_2\\) we would need at least three sunny lines, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines can never exist.  \n   Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -2x + (n+i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because of their distinct intercepts.  \n\n   Fix a diagonal \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \n   Solving the system  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y = -2x + (n+i+1)\\\\[2pt]\n   x+y = s\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   gives  \n\n   \\[\n   x = n+i+1-s,\\qquad y = 2s-n-i-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(x\\) runs through the whole set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}\\) when \\(i\\) runs through