n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n   \\]\n   All these points lie on the line  \n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=-x+n+1 .\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(-1\\), therefore \\(L\\) is **not** sunny.  \n   To obtain a sunny line we replace \\(L\\) by any line that passes through the same set of points and has a slope different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\).  \n   For instance the line\n   \\[\n   L':\\;y=-2x+(2n+1)\n   \\]\n   passes through the same integer points because for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) we have\n   \\[\n   L'\\bigl(t,\\;n+1-t\\bigr):\\quad\n   -2t+(2n+1)=n+1-t .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(L'\\) contains exactly the points \\((t,n+1-t)\\) \\((1\\le t\\le n)\\) and its slope \\(-2\\) is sunny.  \n   Consequently the configuration consisting of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with \\(L'\\) uses exactly one sunny line.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   The three points\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n   \\]\n   belong to \\(T_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Any line that contains two of these points is either horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\)).  \n   All three possibilities are non‑sunny, therefore a **sunny** line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n   To cover the three points we need at least three distinct lines, and at most one of them can be sunny.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=-2x+(n+i+1).\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(-2\\) is sunny, and the lines are distinct because of their different intercepts.  \n   Fix a diagonal \\(s\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (i.e. the set of points with \\(x+y=s\\)).  \n   Solving the system\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y=-2x+(n+i+1)\\\\[2pt]\n   x+y=s\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   x=n+i+1-s,\\;\\;y=2s-n-i-1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(x\\) runs through the whole set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,s-1\\}\\) when \\(i\\) runs through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Consequently the points \\((x,y)\\) obtained from the \\(n\\) lines are exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(x+y=s\\).  \n   Since this holds for every admissible \\(s\\), the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers **all** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Thus a configuration with \\(n\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) – possible (all vertical lines).  \n* \\(k=1\\) – possible (all vertical lines except one sunny line).  \n* \\(k=n\\) – possible (the explicit sunny family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\)).  \n* \\(k=2\\) – impossible (proved).  \n\nThe status of the remaining values \\(k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) is not settled by the arguments presented here.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the values }k=0,1,n\\text{ are attainable, whereas }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
