ion:**  \n- Distinctness: obvious.  \n- Non\u2011sunny: each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n- Coverage: For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (since \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\)), so the point lies on \\(x=a\\).  \nThus the construction works. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Attempted construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n> The only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are those with first coordinate \\(a=n\\); they are \\((n,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  \n> All these points lie on the line \\(L:\\;y=-x+n+1\\).  \n> The slope of \\(L\\) is \\(-1\\), therefore \\(L\\) is **not** sunny.  \n> To obtain a sunny line we replace \\(L\\) by any line that passes through the same set of points and has a slope different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\).  \n> For instance the line \\(L':\\;y=-2x+(2n+1)\\) passes through the same integer points because for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) we have  \n> \\[\n> L'\\bigl(t,\\;n+1-t\\bigr):\\quad -2t+(2n+1)=n+1-t .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification of the claim about the set of uncovered points:**  \nThe points \\((t,n+1-t)\\) for \\(t=1,\\dots,n\\) indeed are exactly those with \\(a+b=n+1\\); they are the \u201canti\u2011diagonal\u201d of the triangle. Correct.\n\n**Verification of the claim that they all lie on \\(L\\):**  \nSubstituting \\((t,n+1-t)\\) into \\(y=-x+n+1\\) gives \\(n+1-t = -t + n+1\\), which holds. So \\(L\\) is the unique line through those points, with slope \\(-1\\).\n\n**Critical error in the replacement step:**  \nAny line that contains **all** points \\((t,n+1-t)\\) must be exactly the line \\(y=-x+n+1\\). The slope is forced to be \\(-1\\). Therefore it is impossible to find a line with a different slope that passes through the *same* set of points. The solution\u2019s statement \u201creplace \\(L\\) by any line that passes through the same set of points and has a slope different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\)\u201d is false.\n\n**Algebraic verification of \\(L'\\):**  \nThe solution asserts that for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\),\n\\[\n-2t+(2n+1)=n+1-t .\n\\]\nSimplifying,\n\\[\n-2t+2n+1 = n+1-t \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; -2t+2n+1 = n+1-t \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; -t + n = 0 \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; t=n .\n\\]\nThus the equality holds **only** when \\(t=n\\). Consequently \\(L'\\) passes through the single point \\((n,1)\\) but not through any of the other \\((t,n+1-t)\\). The claim that \\(L'\\) contains *exactly* those \\(n\\) points is false.\n\n**Conclusion for this part:**  \nThe construction for \\(k=1\\) is invalid. The step contains a **Critical Error** (incorrect algebraic claim and impossible geometric claim). Therefore the statement \u201c\\(k=1\\) is attainable\u201d is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Impossibility claim for \\(k=2\\)\n\n> \u201cThe three points \\(P_1=(1,1),\\;P_2=(1,2),\\;P_3=(2,1)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n> Any line that contains two of these points is either horizontal (\\(y=1\\)), vertical (\\(x=1\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\)).  \n> All three possibilities are non\u2011sunny, therefore a **sunny** line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n> To cover the three points we need at least three distinct lines, and at most one of them can be sunny.  \n> Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification of the geometric facts:**  \n- \\(P_1,P_2\\) lie on \\(x=1\\) (vertical).  \n- \\(P_1,P_3\\) lie on \\(y=1\\) (horizontal).  \n- \\(P_2,P_3\\) lie on the line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which has slope \\((1-2)/(2-1)=-1\\).  \nAll three lines are indeed non\u2011sunny.\n\n**Logical gap:**  \nFrom \u201ca sunny line can contain at most one of the three points\u201d it follows that **among the lines covering those three points** at most one can be sunny. However, the problem does **not** require that the sunny